  :adr/body
  "# ADR-2607184000: Bounded transport ABI and Kotoba provider components

**Status**: accepted
**Date**: 2026-07-18
**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki

## Context

The stack already implements more than an abstract capability design: real
HTTP execution with scope and quota checks, sandboxed filesystem providers,
Kagi non-exportable signing, and an AIUEOS capability syscall trampoline with
ambient syscalls disabled. The missing layer is a lower transport/TLS ABI that
lets protocol providers move into Kotoba without exposing descriptors,
syscall numbers, raw addresses, root secrets, or TLS-disable switches.

## Decision

Introduce five bounded imports: `transport-connect`, `tls-open`,
`transport-write`, `transport-read`, and `transport-close`. Connections are
opaque affine i64 handles; zero is invalid. Connect scope is an exact host and
port, TLS requires an exact server name, and runtime limits default to zero
open connections with finite read/write/connect bounds.

The compiler embeds and validates the canonical ABI manifest. The reference
Kotoba runtime lowers the five operations to real Wasm imports through the
shared core contract. HTTP and database provider prototypes are `.kotoba`
components that export open/read/write/close functions and import only this
bounded surface. Kototama recognizes the surface and accepts explicit
HostFunction bindings through its provider-link seam; if a requested native
provider is absent, it rejects before Wasm instantiation and records a denial.

## Maturity boundary

This is a bounded executable linkage prototype, not production networking.
The native socket/TLS HostFunction provider and general component-to-component
linker remain pending. Existing direct HTTP providers remain the qualified
path. Q9 consumer cutover, three CI runs, seven-day soak and rollback rules are
unchanged.

## Consequences

HTTP/DB protocol logic now has a concrete Kotoba component destination and a
compiler-checked lower ABI. The remaining native implementation is narrowly
specified and cannot silently fall back to ambient network or syscall access.
